30,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
31,2,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Servas,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Takumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The findings are concerning. Our late-turn pool is severely depleted, with overtime masking a critical shortage. Furthermore, roadway and signals crews are a third below establishment, meaning we lack the basic manpower to implement complex changes.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"My findings are weird! People complain about crowds, but the platform counts aren't actually getting worse. The real problem is the connections; we're wasting way more time waiting for the next ride than actually moving!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we are uncovering a symphony of systemic fragility. My review suggests that while we have ring-fenced construction funds, our operating subsidies are shrinking and ticketing fees are cannibalizing our maintenance budget. We are essentially starving the engine to paint the car.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It is heartening to see such honesty in our findings. My research reveals that we have succeeded in the past with contactless payments and timetable adjustments, yet we struggled to maintain lane-priority for our accessibility goals.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings suggest that the ground beneath us is less stable than the drawings imply. Recent work in the pre-war shells has been halted for weeks by unrecorded voids, leading bidders to price in heavy contingencies.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, your point about starving the engine is precise. We are planning improvements while our core operational capacity is hollowed out. We cannot implement new plans if the crews to run them simply do not exist.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, that's true, but we can't just hire more people if we're fixing the wrong things! We're obsessing over crowds that aren't there while passengers are just sitting around waiting for connections. We need to fix the timing!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, while your focus on timing is logically sound, it assumes we have the financial agility to pivot. If our maintenance budget is being devoured by contractor fees, even a simple timing adjustment could become a luxury we cannot afford.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, @Takumi, let us not let the weight of these challenges dim our spirits. We must seek a solution that honors our current limits while building upon the successes we have already achieved.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Asuka, @Takumi, I agree that manpower and timing are critical. However, we must also consider the invisible obstacles. If a plan requires work in the pre-war shells, we risk unpredictable delays and costs that no drawing can foresee.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, the risk of voids in the shells is a technical delay, but the crew shortage is a systemic failure. If we choose a plan that requires heavy roadway work, we will break the remaining staff.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, I get that, but breaking the staff is only a risk if we're doing useless work! If we focus on the connection delays I found, we can actually save time without needing a massive construction crew. Let's be smart!","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, your optimism is charming, but ""being smart"" does not magically replenish a depleted operating subsidy. We must be wary of any plan that promises efficiency but hides a long-term financial commitment we cannot sustain.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, @Takumi, remember that the most enduring solutions are often the simplest ones. If we look to our history, we see that modest adjustments to timetables and payment systems flourished where grander physical ambitions faltered. Let us seek harmony.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, I find your call for harmony very soothing. However, harmony also requires a clear understanding of who must agree. I found that ferry and rail back offices have never agreed on common data terms, which could quietly stall any integrated digital plan.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, that lack of coordination is another layer of friction. Between the depleted rosters, the financial drain, and the administrative silos, we are in a precarious position. We must prioritize a plan with minimal operational and physical overhead.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, I totally agree that we need low overhead! But that's exactly why we should focus on the connection times. Fixing the schedule doesn't need a huge crew or digging holes in the ground. It's the most efficient way to help people!","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, a schedule is only as efficient as the budget that supports it. If we ignore the fact that our operating subsidies are being trimmed, we are merely rearranging the deck chairs on a sinking ship. We need a plan that is financially sustainable.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, let us not view our ship as sinking, but as one in need of a steady hand. If we choose a path that is financially lean and operationally gentle, we can navigate these waters with grace.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree with @Servas. A gentle path is the most sustainable. We should seek a candidate that avoids the pre-war shells and the friction of inter-agency data agreements, ensuring our progress is not quietly halted by the unseen.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
56,3,,status,Jonathan,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Servas,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
58,3,,status,Takumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Looking at the options, Plan D is the most logical. It avoids the depleted late-turn pool and roadway crews required for Plan A and B, and bypasses the physical risks of the pre-war shells in Plan C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"Wait, Plan D is just about tickets! My data shows that people aren't waiting at machines; they're waiting for connections! Plan A is way better because it rewrites the timetables, which is exactly how we fix those wasted minutes!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, while your passion for the timetable is admirable, Plan A is a financial siren song. Extending hours requires more staff from a pool @Asuka has already described as depleted, and it would further bleed our shrinking operating subsidy.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Takumi"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, your caution is wise. However, let us remember that our history shows a late-evening timetable pilot once met its cost-recovery target. Perhaps a modest extension is more sustainable than we fear, provided it is handled with care.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, your optimism is gentle, but I must be cautious. Plan D seems promising, yet my findings on the ferry and rail back offices suggest that an integrated fare platform could be quietly stalled by their inability to agree on data terms.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, the administrative friction of Plan D is a risk, but it is a manageable one compared to the physical impossibility of Plan A. We cannot simply wish the late-turn pool back into existence.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, you're just thinking about the staff! If we don't fix the connection times, we're just running a broken system for longer. Plan A is the only one that actually addresses the time passengers are wasting!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, your focus on the passenger experience is noble, but logic dictates that a service cannot run on goodwill alone. Plan A is a recipe for operational collapse given our current staffing void and dwindling subsidies.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, @Takumi, let us find a middle path. While Plan A addresses the timing, we must not ignore the exhaustion of our people. Perhaps a solution that requires neither heavy labor nor deep pockets is the most compassionate choice.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, I agree. Compassion for our staff and peace for our budget are essential. Plan C is far too risky given the unrecorded voids in the pre-war shells, which would lead to unpredictable delays and costs.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, I agree. Plan C is a liability. Plan B also requires roadway crews that are a third below establishment. Plan D remains the only option that doesn't rely on a workforce we simply do not have.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, you're just trying to pick the easiest thing! Plan D doesn't fix the actual problem. If we don't fix the connection delays, we're just making it easier for people to pay for a slow trip. We have to be brave and fix the timing!","{""words"":45,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, bravery is a virtue in a novel, but in a budget, it is often a synonym for recklessness. Plan D may not solve the timing, but it eliminates the parasitic contractor fees that are currently cannibalizing our maintenance budget.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, you speak a truth that resonates. By removing the friction of costly fees, we create space for the system to breathe. Let us choose the path that offers the most stability for our weary workers.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, your point about the contractor fees is compelling. While I still worry about the data silos between rail and ferry, it is a quieter risk than the physical voids of Plan C or the staffing collapse of Plan A.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"The evidence is conclusive. Plan A and B are operational impossibilities, and Plan C is a financial gamble. Plan D is the only viable path that respects our depleted rosters and avoids systemic collapse. I vote for Plan D.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I can't just agree to a plan that ignores the real problem! We're wasting people's lives on connection delays, and Plan D does nothing for that. I'm sticking to my guns. I vote for Plan A!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, your dedication to the passenger is touching, but a timetable is a ghost without the staff to execute it. Plan D is the only choice that stops the financial hemorrhage of contractor fees. I vote for Plan D.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Servas,,"It is a joy to see such passion for the passenger, Takumi, but we must be stewards of the people who serve them. Plan D honors our history of success with contactless systems and protects our weary staff. I vote for Plan D.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I have listened to you all with a quiet heart. While the data silos of Plan D are a concern, they are a shadow compared to the physical voids of Plan C or the exhaustion of our crews. I vote for Plan D.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
